#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a256e657-9160-5a83-b20d-79cdf7ffa267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146990} {:answer/id #uuid "bd158833-5c20-501c-8971-5c4b3b80a2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146991} {:answer/id #uuid "39dc3ccf-7490-555f-bed1-d94c390a5a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146992} {:answer/id #uuid "40ee66f1-c1e7-5bf9-8e6b-c2998bdefed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146993} {:answer/id #uuid "7765013e-ce19-5ec5-944a-65646a33f791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7765013e-ce19-5ec5-944a-65646a33f791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146994} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d12d0f8-ff1d-530a-89a1-c0aacf3ad4e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8c00e37-f5cb-5639-8ab6-118fe74f0e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-96513" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/d3a06bdc7a63a86651752ff70331010920d24728e7f4e4ad9f067eb860ebaf4b.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-1$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-1.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}-1,$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,1),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3e2e002-3ebb-5c1f-ab3b-003b1ddaced6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96516-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147008} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb091d1-5143-5e35-8ca4-0659908bca9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96516-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147009} {:answer/id #uuid "30e39afd-8e84-556b-ab67-c96815516570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96516-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147010} {:answer/id #uuid "49a2cdb5-c159-512a-9d77-59fddf42d6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96516-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147011} {:answer/id #uuid "540d9724-512c-56ce-9ca3-b3d309629b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96516-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e2e002-3ebb-5c1f-ab3b-003b1ddaced6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96516-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147008} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb002cc-f020-5cc7-afd2-54c861525e04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a73138f3-4685-5359-a263-5ec8c82467dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96516" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/b958bd4db4353cfe7a852b79f7144d0b48b85c48b3d97f813b09db2858ec110a.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-3,y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-3,0,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/666ae97e41e825161c68cc6146a06c70b87c6d84b11ae8116c0d415c340987fa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a44e6b-d2f8-5484-9cde-1ed8a7547b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/73994f96f68cd43ee0377ea2005479e0c927e37dc0a15e2e9da35c1c8900afa0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341126} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0bb362-7aa5-567b-863a-70f246ba8470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/78373cbb2e323dcbfbc40770fb47a59939670ca8e27a70b3ef19eaa04ba3f939.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341127} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd8ec8d-cbfa-51d1-b0aa-9aff0445243a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b5796afbbcf6ec35a0980a82957ddbf74813b0113b621a0b77cfc24f8706838.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341128} {:answer/id #uuid "83108880-be61-5974-b590-83d266bc5024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b51f096104fe981a341096c945881595529d17061d0e5656e1ef3d1a9f79df4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341129} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7f20b9-f7b9-5fbf-bbac-f7cf8d5048df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/e17edbcca21405dde82e9b87ad0f870fa3e4383572785210d5b63cc706f57897.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83108880-be61-5974-b590-83d266bc5024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b51f096104fe981a341096c945881595529d17061d0e5656e1ef3d1a9f79df4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341129} :answer-field/id #uuid "165d44d5-4225-5c4d-b377-bfddc521721c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f487ce6-961a-5d12-8ceb-0da56b2d670b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97915" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/25d8d89ac4fdb3937596606d3ac347e08e3492c3dca65c3886c6eca8e3e121b5.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-1,y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore $y=-1,0,2$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b51f096104fe981a341096c945881595529d17061d0e5656e1ef3d1a9f79df4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892" :knowledge-point/title "Phase Lines and Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093b88c7-ea2c-58fd-a8b2-bde1e7dee6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/d46036d0f5c0fc689d1123e3512a446b94688f6fa94df017ee26682860ed6b64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341133} {:answer/id #uuid "3b41e1bd-5d3a-5001-90ed-633a34435d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/03d9436c922b602b3e3388dfc21b6e8c20aae96f70b378de706b3c96b7ded253.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341134} {:answer/id #uuid "ba82f68b-7f08-5823-8363-0993b3caaf7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/86bcece5228ba30310e4b4ed663c5efcb551609474fa924034efac08aa055a23.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341135} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5386a3-bea2-5aaf-acca-f6f751e65352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f95aa207248151fc7035fcd6dcdea8de58ebff368aa886b18413249bf30225ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341136} {:answer/id #uuid "1d62cd27-e989-5d8e-84ab-cc994623c357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f22dc056accfeae38eea6fb0207adfe4676bd8bd875cbeb96c078f4eb6457a6e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d62cd27-e989-5d8e-84ab-cc994623c357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f22dc056accfeae38eea6fb0207adfe4676bd8bd875cbeb96c078f4eb6457a6e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341137} :answer-field/id #uuid "13325c0c-2256-5a30-ba60-d90a721de45c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b7cd7a6-7b0b-599b-8ee5-35ee0fead25b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98040" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/58369eb9878aba12bf52d22a930ec348e7b6cf31e1b2c8d77823de1aab5d9c87.png)\n\nThe slope field of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line of the given equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y=-2$ and $y=0$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,0),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/fcc9f998111493167284cf5844645ac2201a230b073a9336604fb790ba16bad4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57fe8823-70c4-5f0b-a359-9ad3fe8f4b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341140} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6e8826-3851-5ddc-ab3c-5ecdb6c30c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341141} {:answer/id #uuid "c27fff79-8109-54bd-a3ee-63b85cc9ce83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341142} {:answer/id #uuid "94e03541-5d84-5fdb-96c1-709122d1374d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341143} {:answer/id #uuid "e541cd39-0d40-55cf-91a7-09d8a31aeb06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c27fff79-8109-54bd-a3ee-63b85cc9ce83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341142} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0769a07-0b20-52e2-b52d-b895a1561a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b30c866d-fa3d-5692-b249-cd39aee84dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-98632" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
